From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] linker: search the VNDK APEX when there is no ld.config.txt

A Halium GSI ships no /system/etc/ld.config.txt, so every process falls back
to init_default_namespace_no_config() and gets kDefaultLdPaths. That already
names two APEXes - 996d3ef added com.android.runtime for bionic, d792760 added
com.android.i18n - but both are single fixed directories, and the VNDK is a
third one that neither covers.

It cannot be added the same way, because the directory carries the vendor's
VNDK version: com.android.vndk.v30 against an Android 11 vendor,
com.android.vndk.v33 against a 13 one. So scan /apex rather than naming it.

Without this a stock Android 11 vendor's libraries do not load. The Pixel 3a's
audio HAL links android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so, which is shipped
nowhere but /apex/com.android.vndk.v30/lib64:

    library "android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so" not found
    E droid-util.c: Failed to load audio hw module audio.primary :
        Invalid argument (22)

and pulseaudio dies on module-droid-card. Stock Android reaches that directory
through the "vndk" namespace ld.config.txt declares and links the vendor
namespace to; with no config file there is no such namespace and no link.

The result is appended after the built-in paths, so /system, /odm and /vendor
keep precedence.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
--- a/common/q/linker.cpp
+++ b/common/q/linker.cpp
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <android/api-level.h>
+#include <dirent.h>
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <inttypes.h>
@@ -4154,6 +4155,47 @@
     }
   }
 
+  // Add whatever VNDK APEX is mounted.
+  //
+  // A Halium GSI ships no /system/etc/ld.config.txt, so this function -- not
+  // the configured path below -- is what every process ends up with.
+  // kDefaultLdPaths already names two APEXes, com.android.runtime for bionic
+  // and com.android.i18n, but both are fixed directories. The VNDK APEX is
+  // named for the vendor's VNDK version -- com.android.vndk.v30 against an
+  // Android 11 vendor, v33 against a 13 one -- so it cannot be a constant and
+  // has to be looked up.
+  //
+  // Without it a stock Android 11 vendor's libraries do not load: the Pixel
+  // 3a's audio HAL links android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so, which
+  // exists nowhere but /apex/com.android.vndk.v30/lib64, so it fails with
+  //
+  //     library "android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so" not found
+  //
+  // and every consumer of that HAL dies with it. Stock Android reaches the
+  // same directory through the "vndk" namespace that ld.config.txt sets up;
+  // with no config there is no such namespace and no link to it.
+  //
+  // Appended after the built-in paths, so /system, /odm and /vendor still win.
+  DIR* apex_dir = opendir("/apex");
+  if (apex_dir != nullptr) {
+    static const char kVndkPrefix[] = "com.android.vndk.v";
+    for (dirent* entry = readdir(apex_dir); entry != nullptr; entry = readdir(apex_dir)) {
+      if (strncmp(entry->d_name, kVndkPrefix, sizeof(kVndkPrefix) - 1) != 0) {
+        continue;
+      }
+      std::string vndk_lib_dir = std::string("/apex/") + entry->d_name +
+#if defined(__LP64__)
+                                 "/lib64";
+#else
+                                 "/lib";
+#endif
+      if (realpath(vndk_lib_dir.c_str(), real_path) != nullptr) {
+        ld_default_paths.push_back(real_path);
+      }
+    }
+    closedir(apex_dir);
+  }
+
   g_default_namespace->set_default_library_paths(std::move(ld_default_paths));
 
   std::vector<android_namespace_t*> namespaces;
